  "$comment": "Rung 1 of the trust ladder: hand-verified values that beat every automated source. Every row with `vendorVerified` carries the `verifiedUrl` that was actually read, and the date it was read — that is what makes the claim checkable rather than merely asserted. Anthropic and OpenAI rows were reconciled against the first-party pricing pages on 2026-08-01; both matched what the automated feed already published, and the additions here are the modifiers the feed does not carry (cache-write rates, long-context tiers, batch discounts). `capabilityIndex` is an illustrative 0-100 placeholder used only for the value map's Y axis - it is NOT a benchmark score, models without one are simply not plotted, and the UI says so.",

  "$displayNames": "Some entries carry a `displayName` and nothing else. Those are NOT price claims — they deliberately omit `vendorVerified`, so the rate and its provenance keep coming from whichever source the trust ladder chose. They exist because upstream ids get title-cased into names like `GPT 5`, `O3 Mini` and `Qwen3 Vl 235b A22b Instruct`, and since every model now has a public page those strings are its <h1> and its search-result title. A name is presentation; correcting one must not quietly upgrade a price to `vendor`.",

